  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Sunday)\nIssued at 1215 PM CST MON NOV 9 2026\n\nQuasi-zonal flow aloft continues through Wednesday with moderating\ntemperatures. Several vorticity maxima originating from a\nsouthern-stream upper low over Texas will move through the area\nTuesday through Wednesday afternoon. This increase in mid and\nupper level moisture will result in mainly some to mid to high\nlevel clouds, with dry air in place at low-levels. However, there\nmay be some virga/sprinkles at times, with a low chance for light\nrain, since models can sometimes under-do QPF in these \nsituations. Have kept chances for measurable rain around 10 \npercent for now during the Tuesday night - Wednesday afternoon\ntimeframe. \n\nA strong northern-stream shortwave trough will bring a clipper-\nlike system and strong cold front into the area Wednesday night \ninto Thursday. Low-level moisture return will be lacking ahead of \nthe front with dew points mainly in the low to mid 50s, but strong\nforcing and steep mid-level lapse rates will be sufficient for a \nfew showers. There may be just enough instability for an isolated \nthunderstorm, but severe storms are not expected at this time. A \nmuch cooler air mass moves in behind the front, with highs in the \n50s and high chances for the first freeze of the season across at \nleast northern portions of the area. \n\n32/JDavis",
